REPORT CARD GRADER

Report Card Grader — a real-world task using only today's concepts: comparisons (==, !=, <, >, <=, >=), if / elif / else, variables, input(), int() conversion, basic arithmetic, and printing. No imports, no tricks.

Solve it individually, then come together as a group to compare approaches. Good luck!

The same class teacher from Friday's report card task is tired of grading by hand. She wants a program that grades one subject score for a student using her school's scale, and tells each student exactly how far they are from the next grade.

Her grading scale:

- 70 to 100: A - Excellent
- 60 to 69: B - Very Good
- 50 to 59: C - Good
- 45 to 49: D - Pass
- 0 to 44: F - Fail

The program must:
- Ask for the student's name and one subject score (a whole number).
- If the score is not a real score (above 100 or below 0), print a clear message and no grade at all.
- Print one line in this shape: Adaeze scored 73: B (Very Good)
- Then print how many more marks the student needs to reach the next grade, for example: 7 more marks to reach A. A student on grade A has nothing left to chase, so handle that differently.

Sample run:

Student name: Adaeze
Score: 73

Adaeze scored 73: B (Very Good)
7 more marks to reach A

Another run:

Student name: Tunde
Score: 30

Tunde scored 30: F (Fail)
15 more marks to reach D

Edge cases to handle: exactly 70, exactly 69, a score of 0, a score of 100, a score of 105, a score of -5, and a student who is already on grade A.
